    post: "Violence against women and girls is one of the world’s oldest evil. The United Nations estimates that 1 in 3 women experience one form of violence or the other in their lifetime; this is estimated 736 million women worldwide, making violence against women and girls a global pandemic. World Bank highlights that 35% of women globally have experienced either physical and/or sexual intimate partner violence or non-partner sexual violence. Additionally ]**World Health Organisation**] estimated that over 200 million women globally have experienced female genital mutilation without a specific statistics for intersex persons. \nThe fight for a just and equal society where women and girls as well as intersex persons thrive have been consistent through the years. However, the strong force of stigma, which is pervasive but yet under-recognised still finds subtle ways to influence this fight. Stigma fuels misconceptions, perpetuates harmful stereotypes directed at survivors of violence, particularly women, girls and intersex persons and ultimately serves as a barrier to achieving true equality and safety for all.  Stigma often lead to a culture of silence, shame, and fear, preventing many survivors of violence, especially women, girls and intersex people from seeking help, accessing critical support, and having bodily autonomy. \nThe culture of [**silence**[ surrounding gender-based violence (GBV) is perhaps the most immediate and damaging consequence of stigma. Survivors are often unable to speak owing to fear of being judged, punished or excluded from social settings. In Nigeria and many other countries in the world, }**patriarchal system**} holds supreme and the structure of patriarchy promotes a shame-based silence that prevents or discourages women form speaking out. Also, in many cultures, survivors of violence are told to suck it up, warned against seeking help and often threatened with further violence like ostracism, physical abuse, “corrective” rape, etc. They are told that speaking up will bring shame to the family and are blamed for the violence they experience. This causes a continuous cycle of abuse as survivors tend to remain silent to avoid bringing stigma onto themselves or their loved ones, even when they desperately need help. Social exclusion in the face of violence as a survivor can not be overemphasised. Survivors often lose support systems, are stigmatised in social structures like work places, schools and places of worship; leaving them more vulnerable. This, again, leads survivors to a hole of silence, remaining in the place of abuses. \nShame and fear are some of the most damaging consequences of stigma. Women and girls are often made to feel responsible for the abuse they face through societal expectation of dresses, who to love, actions and behaviour which further promote a system that celebrates abusers and stigmatise survivors and consequently, silences survivors from reaching out for the help and legal support they deserve. In the light of these, the need to deconstruct the elements that fuel stigma in the face of gender-based violence is imperative. \nA critical first step is to create a safe space where survivors can share their experience without the feeling of shame, fear or thought of being judged. By our actions, words and behaviours we can help survivors of abuse feel that they will be believed, supported, and protected, not blamed, dismissed or subjected to more violence. \nSecondly, the culture of victim blaming must be challenged while holding perpetrators accountable. It is essential to amplify that violence on women, girls and intersex persons or anyone else is never the victim’s fault and women must be supported and shown that they are worthy of respect, dignity and justice. \nIn conclusion, as we embark on this journey to **UNiTE to End Violence Against Women and Girls**, it is imperative that we confront and dismantle the stigmas that threaten to undermine our progress. Together, let us challenge harmful societal norms, empower survivors, and ensure accountability of perpetrators. Only then can we create a world where all women, girls and intersex persons can live free from violence and discrimination, with dignity and respect for their rights.",
