    text: "In the fight against gender-based violence, the experiences of intersex people are often overlooked, hidden in the shadows of the binary gender world. Intersex people, born with variations in sex characteristics that don’t fit typical male or female norms, face a unique set  of injustices that go largely unaddressed.",
    description:
      "Intersex persons face unique gender-based violence and injustice. Learn about their struggles, the fight for dignity, and the path to equality and justice",
    mainImg:
      "https://res.cloudinary.com/dcjebpvia/image/upload/f_auto/v1751211838/intersex-ng-images/articles/hidden-realities.jpg",
    post: "In the fight against gender-based violence, the experiences of intersex people are often overlooked, hidden in the shadows of the binary gender world. Intersex people, born with variations in sex characteristics that don’t fit typical male or female norms, face a unique set  of injustices that go largely unaddressed. During this year’s 16 Days of Activism, it’s time to bring their stories to light and demand protection, dignity, and respect for intersex individuals everywhere. \nDespite feminist strides, ours is still not a society that supports and rewards individuals who do not conform to gender norms. Little boys are still kept in line with phrases like, “Don’t be a sissy.” Little girls and particularly older girls face fierce disapproval if they behave or dress too “boyishly.” This early punishment for simply expressing gender identity leaves many scars, but the experiences that lead  intersexual domestic violence survivors to believe that it’s normal for “people like me” to live with abuse only increase in magnitude as the intersex survivor matures. \n**The Invisible Violence of “Correction”** \nFor many intersex persons, gender-based violence begins at birth. Doctors, often with the backing of family members, perform non-consensual “normalizing” surgeries on infants and young children. These surgeries aim to make a child’s body conform to societal expectations of male or female. The procedures may involve removing or altering genital tissue, reshaping organs, or administering hormones to “correct the body” irreversibly. \nThe paper titled Trans and Intersex Survivors of Domestic Violence: Defining Terms, Barriers, & Responsibilities By Diana Courvant and Loree Cook-Daniels talks about a doctor who at an annual convention of the Society for the Scientific Study of Sexuality related the case of a girl child with a large clitoris sexually mutilated by her father, who was angered at the phallic proportions of his infant daughter’s clitoris. Amazingly, this doctor completed the child’s mutilation by performing a clitoridectomy and was using her story to justify surgery on infants in similar situations, rather than healing the child and calling attention to her abuse. Although these stories’ power is anecdotal and not statistical, they and others like them are widely known and retold among intersex individuals. Because of the extreme cruelty and casual indifference of authorities and institutions exemplified in these common stories, an intersex survivor may fear an unknown service institution more than a familiar abuser. \n**A Life in the Shadows: The Psychological Toll of Secrecy and Stigma** \nThe psychological violence against intersex persons doesn’t end with childhood surgeries. From a young age, many are shamed, silenced, or pressured to keep their identities a secret. Imagine growing up in a world that insists you’re “different” in ways that make people uncomfortable. Families, often unprepared to understand or advocate for intersex rights, can unintentionally fuel this isolation. \nOne U.S.- based intersex advocate described feeling “invisible” for years. “My parents tried to hide it from me,” they explained, “and so I felt like there was something wrong with me. It took years of unlearning and self-love to realize that I wasn’t broken.” The shame imposed by society often leads to mental health issues like depression, anxiety, and in extreme cases, suicidal ideation. \n**Intersex People at the Margins of Rights** \nInstitutional discrimination compounds the trauma, as many countries including Nigeria don’t legally recognize intersex identities or protect intersex rights. Denied recognition and often forced to choose a binary gender, intersex individuals encounter obstacles in healthcare, legal systems, and workplaces. In Kenya, until 2020, intersex people couldn’t obtain identification that accurately reflected their identity, preventing access to basic services like education and healthcare. This legal erasure is yet another form of violence, keeping intersex people trapped in systems that deny their existence and rights. \n**The Road to Dignity: What Needs to Change** \nTo end this cycle of gender based violence, we must confront the medical, social, and legal systems that violate the rights of intersex persons. Here’s how we can begin to create a safer world for intersex persons: \n1.	**End Non-Consensual Surgeries:** All children, including intersex children, have a right to bodily autonomy. We must urge medical systems to halt unnecessary surgeries on intersex infants and ensure that any medical interventions are based on consent and necessity, not outdated ideals of “normal.” \n2.	**Raise Awareness and Erase Stigma:** The shame and isolation imposed on intersex persons by society need to end. Through education, communities can learn that intersex characteristics are natural, diverse variations of human biology. Awareness campaigns can help shift the narrative from secrecy to acceptance. \n3.	**Advocate for Legal Protections and Recognition:** Governments must create laws that prohibit discrimination against intersex individuals and recognize intersex identities. Legal protections and rights are crucial to ensure that intersex people can live without fear of exclusion, discrimination, or violence. \n4.	**Center Intersex Voices:** Change will only be meaningful if intersex individuals are included in conversations about their own rights and welfare. From activism to policy making, intersex voices should lead the way in creating a society that celebrates, rather than hides, their identities.\n**A Call to Action** \nAs we participate in the 16 Days of Activism, let us not forget intersex people, whose stories are often silenced. Their fight for dignity, autonomy, and justice is as vital as any in the movement against gender-based violence. Stand with intersex persons. Share their stories. Demand an end to practices that violate their rights. Together, we can create a world where no one is forced to hide, and everyone’s identity is respected and protected. \nIt’s time to shine a light on these hidden realities and make room for all identities in the global call for equality and justice.",
